Key Characteristics of Static Pods

	Feature					Static Pod

	Managed by				Kubelet
	Created via				Node filesystem
	Scheduled by			Not Scheduler
	Managed by Deployment	No
	API Server control		No
	Runs on					Specific node only

Where Static Pods Are Stored
	
	Static pod manifests are usually stored in:

		/etc/kubernetes/manifests

	Kubelet continuously watches this directory.

	Example:

	/etc/kubernetes/manifests
	    kube-apiserver.yaml
	    kube-controller-manager.yaml
	    kube-scheduler.yaml
	    etcd.yaml


How Static Pods Work

	Step-by-step workflow:

		1️⃣ Kubelet watches a directory.

		/etc/kubernetes/manifests

		2️⃣ When a YAML file appears:

		nginx-pod.yaml

		3️⃣ Kubelet reads the file.

		4️⃣ Kubelet instructs container runtime to start the container.

		5️⃣ Pod starts running.

		6️⃣ A Mirror Pod is created in API Server for visibility.

		6️⃣ Mirror Pods

	Even though static pods are created by Kubelet, Kubernetes still shows them in the cluster.

	It creates a Mirror Pod.

		Example:

		kubectl get pods -n kube-system

		Output:

		kube-apiserver-master
		kube-scheduler-master
		kube-controller-manager-master

		These are actually static pods.

Static Pod Creation Example


	Step 1 Create YAML File
		
		apiVersion: v1
		kind: Pod
		metadata:
		  name: nginx-static
		spec:
		  containers:
		  - name: nginx
		    image: nginx
		    ports:
		    - containerPort: 80
	Step 2 Move File to Static Pod Directory
		
		sudo cp nginx.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
	
	Step 3 Kubelet Automatically Creates Pod

	Check:

		kubectl get pods

	Output:

		nginx-static-node1


Static Pod Naming

	Static pod names include node name automatically.

Example:

	nginx-static-node01

Reason:

	Pods are tied to that specific node.

